I have to say, I like machine learning too, but it would be totally
inappropriate for a Nethack bot.  Any bot basically has to incode an
input/output function: given what you know about the state of the
world, what button do you press?  The problem for learning Nethack is
that this function is madly complex.

>From the very beginning of the game, there's almost nothing for an
automatic learner to grasp.  Consider the problem of learning to
defeat a grid bug.  With well over a thousand characters on the screen
and dozens of possible commands, how do you ever learn by trial and
error that the pair "x@" appearing anywhere on the map means "press
left arrow"?

Encoding objects as you describe helps (and any bot would have to do
this regardless of implementation), but not nearly enough.  The space
of possible inputs is still too immense.  In the situation above, the
input to the function becomes something like: 

I have a +0 scalpel.  I have 5 apples.  I don't have speed boots.  I
don't have a white gem. [repeat for all items]

There is no altar in this room.  There is gold in this room. There is
an open door in this room. [repeat for all room and level
characteristics]

I have poison resistance.  I have 16 charisma.  I know "healing".  I
don't know "sleep". [repeat for all player characteristics]

There is a grid bug nearby.  There is a monster to my left.  There is
a grid bug to my left.  There is no visible monster above me.  There
is no visible monster two spaces to my left. [repeat for all relevant
spaces and note that "grid bug" could be replaced by any monster name]

So... how do you figure out which inputs are relevant when?  Perhaps
you do trial-and-error over many games.  Very occasionally there will
come a turn where a grid bug is to your left.  There are dozens of
possible commands, but every once in a while the "press left arrow"
one will be chosen, and sometimes this will cause the grid bug to die.
Suppose we realize this is a good thing.  The problem is, how do you
know that out of your thousands of input features, only "monster/grid
bug to my left" indicate that "press left arrow" caused this good
thing?

In practice, you don't.  There are so many features of the input that
dozens of them are going to correlate with every event more or less by
chance.  Even if the grid bug scenario comes up every thousand turns,
it won't make a statistical dent in the things that are being learned
on the other 999 turns.  For example, if your pet kills the bug while
you eat an apple, a learner will notice that the "eat" command was
correlated with the success.  If more bugs die when you're a
barbarian, it may suppose that wearing ring mail causes bug death.

And that's just learning to kill a grid bug in an empty room, and I
don't see any way to simplify the problem without throwing away
important input parameters.  For these reasons I think I can say with
some conviction that machine learning will have no place in a
successful Nethack bot.

